            <ArticleTitle>[Rare differential diagnosis on suspected liver tumor].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>612-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00108-008-2278-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>This case report describes a 77-year old woman with a leiomyosarcoma of subhepatic inferior caval vein. The presented symptom was unspecific abdominal pain. Ultrasound and CT suggested a primary liver tumor. MRI revealed retroperitoneal location of the neoplasm. The tumor was resected completely and ICV was replaced by a PTF-graft. Vascular leiomyosarcoma is a rare tumor entity, which often is diagnosed in advanced stage. Depending on location it can be mistaken for neoplasms of other organs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
